-- title:
--   Advancing one complete graph-sketch layer on native arrays
-- statement:
--   Let $0\le i<k-1$, and assume the native core state contains the dense graph-sketch matrix $D_{i+1}$, graph database, trial signs, and canonical templates. Let the layer index be $i$ and the four vertex, state, and predecessor loop counters be empty. The later-layer program changes only the previous-matrix bank:
--   $$\operatorname{Prev}_{\rm after}=\operatorname{frames}\bigl(D_{i+2}(v,a):v<n,\ a<k\bigr),\qquad t\le\operatorname{laterLayerCost}(n,k,|A|).$$
--   Here $D_p$ is the $2^k$-coordinate exterior recurrence for $p$-vertex walks and the trial's shared signs. The explicit cost sums all $n$ vertex computations, template copying, clearing the old matrix, and transferring the new one. This is a complete native transition of the recursive sketch.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/LaterLayer.lean, line 22; source snapshot SHA-256 e311bc42d359c3b0cb81f6ceed80c429bf918d2a37a339bd7485381e08e00100.

